 **Parameters:**
-- $n$: The side length of the square blocks into which the pixel set is partitioned. One pixel is processed per $n \times n$ block on the first pass [S1].
-- $M$: The user-selected maximum number of equal-length intervals into which the integration domain is divided. The G-buffer stores $M$ parallel textures [S1].
+*   $n$: Side length of square blocks. One pixel processed per $n \times n$ block. Typical values not explicitly given, but $n=2$ is used in examples [S1].
+*   $M$: Maximum number of equal-length intervals. User-selected. Typical values not explicitly given, but $M=3$ is used in examples [S1].
 
 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-- **Woodgrain artifacts:** Caused by high derivatives of the opacity function near the ray start [S1]. Mitigated by finding the starting point $v$ where the transfer function becomes non-zero, rather than starting at the ray origin [S1]. This also increases the probability that the first integrals $I_0$ coincide on adjacent rays [S1].
-- **Last interval length:** The last interval of integration may be shorter than the others because the ray may exit the volume before completing the $M$-th equal interval [S1]. This is handled by a separate 'lastStep' calculation [S1].
-- **GPU Efficiency:** Unlike recursive block division methods which are inefficient on GPUs due to concurrent processing groups, this fixed two-pass approach avoids the overhead of recursive subdivision [S1].
+*   **Woodgrain Artifacts:** Caused by high derivatives of the opacity function near the ray start. Mitigated by finding the starting point $v$ where the transfer function becomes non-zero, rather than starting at the ray origin [S1].
+*   **Last Interval Length:** The last interval may be shorter than others if the ray exits the volume before completing the $M$-th interval. This is handled by clamping $t_{end}$ to the ray exit point [S1].
+*   **Precision:** Interpolation accuracy depends on the smoothness of the volume and the threshold $\tau$. If neighbors are too different, interpolation fails, and partial integration is required [S1].
 
