 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Accelerates rendering by reducing the length of the interval of integration [S1]. On average, it accelerates adaptive undersampling by a factor of 1.5 [S1].
-- **Measured Performance:** In a real dataset example, 91.7% of pixels were interpolated, 5.7% required 1 interval calculation, 2.1% required 2, 0.1% required 3, and 0.4% required >3 intervals [S1].
-- **Scaling:** The method scales with the viewport resolution and the number of intervals $M$. The G-buffer resolution is $n$ times less than the viewport along each side [S1].
-- **Strengths:** Avoids recursive subdivision overhead on GPUs; reduces computation for most pixels via interpolation; handles high-opacity gradients effectively by starting integration at the non-zero transfer function domain [S1].
-- **Limitations:** Requires two passes; performance depends on the coherence of adjacent pixels; requires storing $M$ parallel textures in the G-buffer [S1].
-- **When to use:** When rendering volumetric data on GPUs where recursive algorithms are inefficient, and when high frame rates are needed with acceptable interpolation artifacts [S1].
-- **When not to use:** When the volume has high-frequency details that are not coherent between adjacent pixels, leading to frequent interval recomputation [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Reduces the number of full ray casts by a factor of $n^2$ in the first pass. The second pass performs interpolation for most pixels (91.7% in one example) and partial integration for the rest [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires a G-buffer with $M+1$ textures of resolution $\frac{W}{n} \times \frac{H}{n}$.
+*   **Performance:** Accelerates adaptive undersampling by a factor of 1.5 on average [S1]. In a specific dataset, 91.7% of pixels were interpolated, 5.7% required 1 interval calculation, 2.1% required 2, 0.1% required 3, and 0.4% required >3 intervals [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Avoids recursive subdivision overhead on GPUs; ensures only necessary intervals are integrated on the second pass [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Requires two passes; interpolation may introduce artifacts if the threshold is not tuned correctly or if geometry changes rapidly [S1].
+*   **When to use:** When rendering volumetric data on GPUs where recursive algorithms are inefficient, and high FPS is required with acceptable quality loss [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Adaptive Undersampling was proposed in the paper "Fast Adaptive Undersampling for Volume Rendering" by Smirnova and Shubnikov (2019) [S1]. It addresses the computational expense of direct volume rendering, where running time is proportional to the number of pixels [S1]. The method improves upon earlier two-pass algorithms by pre-computing interval integrals, allowing for more efficient partial integration in the second pass [S1].
+Originated in the paper "Fast Adaptive Undersampling for Volume Rendering" by Smirnova and Shubnikov (2019) [S1]. It addresses the computational expense of direct volume rendering, which is proportional to the number of pixels [S1]. Previous methods like recursive block division were inefficient on GPUs [S1]. This method builds on earlier two-pass algorithms [L15] and optimizations for saving integral values in ray intervals [BSM18], but improves upon them by using equal-length intervals and a fixed two-pass structure [S1].
 
